SentinelOne's monitoring advice covers new runner registrations and unusual runner names, pipelines created or changed by service accounts, jobs touching unfamiliar repositories, tunnel-opening workflows and suspicious child processes, credential access, reverse tunnels and persistence on build runners; it names no audit-event type, source-address criterion or specific binaries. The post walks through examples rather than a taxonomy of three cases, and attributes rogue self-hosted runners and comment-triggered workflows to Sha1-Hulud. The TeamCity case concerns CVE-2023-42793, fixed in TeamCity On-Premises 2023.05.4, whose pre-patch backdoors JetBrains says persist after patching.
